  "details": "==================================================\n=======HOW TO ADD JUMP ANIMATIONS FOR DUMMIES=======\n==================================================\n\nStep 1.) Get a squat animation that lasts < 1 second. (jumping)\n\nStep 2.) Put the jump trigger on a delay for the duration of the animation.\n\nStep 3.) When the avatar reunites with the floor, play the animation in reverse. (landing)\n_________________________________________________________________________\nNote: As with the walking animations, it would also be ideal to include separate animations for jumping at 0 45, 90, 135, and 180 degrees for when there is directional input in addition to jumping. _________________________________________________________________________\nThat being said, the dev team doesn't strike me as the kind of people willing to go the extra mile by adding MORE animations... or the first mile for that matter. (VRChat had a $4.5 million budget.) \n\nBut don't worry, I did the hard part FOR you. (Just like the rest of the community.)\n\nHere is a beginners tutorial for how to code an animation to play in reverse when a condition is met. Now all you have to do is download an animation from mixamo, and copy and paste the code from the tutorial.\n---> gyanendushekhar.com/2016/10/28/reverse-animation-play-unity3d/\n\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nCongratulations devs! You can now claim that you are working on the VRChat beyond small bug fixes, and cosmetic changes to the UI.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nBy the way, development of a video game that features\n\n- FPS mechanics with Melee combat\n- Puzzle/Platforming elements\n- State of the art visuals\n- Crossplatforming\n- Modding tools\n- MMO support\n\nCould be done with a budget of ~$3.5 million and still have considerable change.",
